## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design dynamic, policy-driven routing with on-edge latency constraints, multi-tenant safeguards, and auditable provenance. It also probes risk scoring, drift detection, and rollback semantics under real-world operational pressures.

## Key Concepts

- Contextual bandits for real-time routing decisions
- Per-tenant safety profiles and data locality constraints
- Latency budgets and SLA adherence (p95 targets)
- Drift detection and automated rollback to baseline
- Tamper-evident, cryptographically signed per-tenant audit logs

## Code Example

```javascript
function selectVariant(context, stats) {
  // context: {tenant, latency, safetyProfile}
  // stats: {risk, base, tuned, safety}
  if (Math.random() < 0.1) return 'base';
  if (stats.risk > 0.6) return 'safety-guarded';
  const scores = { base: stats.base, tuned: stats.tuned, safety: stats.safety };
  return Object.keys(scores).reduce((a,b) => scores[a] > scores[b] ? a : b);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you prevent feedback loops from online learning contaminating production signals?
- What telemetry and signing strategy ensures tamper resistance without leaking tenant data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design cost- and latency-aware routing in multi-tenant LLM ops, including dynamic model selection and prompt segmentation under burst load.

## Key Concepts
- SLA-driven routing and per-tenant budgets
- Dynamic model variant selection (cost vs. latency)
- Prompt chunking strategies and batching
- Telemetry, drift detection, and resilience under burst traffic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: select a model variant based on tenant budget and latency target
function selectVariant(tenant, state, latencyTarget) {
  const budgetLeft = state[tenant].budget - state[tenant].spent;
  const withinLatency = state[tenant].avgLatency <= latencyTarget;
  if (budgetLeft > 0 && withinLatency) return 'highQuality';
  if (budgetLeft > 0) return 'balanced';
  return 'cheap';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you ensure fairness when tenants with large budgets dominate capacity?
- How would you validate latency targets under sudden burst traffic?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates ability to translate privacy and safety requirements into a concrete, low-overhead component that precedes LLM calls and manages per-tenant policy enforcement.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy surface and versioning
- Lightweight, rule-based prompt classification
- Redaction of PII and disallowed content
- Caching decisions to minimize latency and repeated processing

## Code Example
```javascript
function classifyPrompt(prompt) {
  const p = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (/ssn|credit card|password/.test(p)) return 'PII';
  if (p.includes('invoice') || p.includes('order')) return 'TRANSACTION';
  return 'GENERAL';
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical reliability design for LLM gateways, focusing on per-tenant isolation, simple fault handling, and testability—core beginner-to-intermediate LLm-ops concerns.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant circuit breakers and state management
- Retry with exponential backoff and jitter
- Graceful failover to cached/cheaper models
- Lightweight data models and testability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight per-tenant circuit breaker skeleton
class CircuitBreaker {
  constructor(threshold=5, windowMs=60000) {
    this.threshold = threshold; this.windowMs = windowMs;
    this.failures = {}; // tenantId -> [timestamps]
  }
  recordFailure(tid){
    const now = Date.now();
    this.failures[tid] = (this.failures[tid]||[]).concat(now);
  }
  isOpen(tid){
    const now = Date.now();
    const arr = (this.failures[tid]||[]).filter(t=>now-t<=this.windowMs);
    return arr.length >= this.threshold;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor per-tenant circuit states at scale?
- How would you reset a circuit breaker after a long outage without data loss?

## Why This Is Asked
Governance and trust gaps exist in multi-tenant LLM-ops. Provenance and attribution are critical for audits, compliance, and drift detection across tenants.

## Key Concepts
- Data provenance, model attribution, tamper-evident logging, append-only storage
- Per-tenant data contracts and data locality requirements
- Merkle proofs, cryptographic hashing, replayability

## Code Example
```javascript
const crypto = require('crypto');
function logEvent(event){
  const rec = JSON.stringify(event);
  const hash = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(rec).digest('hex');
  appendToWormStore({ ...event, hash, ts: Date.now() });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale the provenance store to 10M events/day?
- How do you enforce tamper-evidence across regional replicas?
